<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Duke of Zhou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> · Also known as Ji Dan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Duke of Zhou, Ji Dan, was a brother of King Wu, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a>. After Wu's early death he acted as regent for the young King Cheng, crushed the rebellion of the Three Guards and the Shang prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-geng">Wu Geng</a>, and organised the granting of new states such as Lu, Wey and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> to royal kinsmen and allies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> revered him as the model minister and the founder of Zhou ritual.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou.jpg" alt="Duke of Zhou" width="320" height="473" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duke_of_Zhou.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
